<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS entitlements for Notesnook Vue.

  The app is NOT sandboxed (no `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`): the desktop
  build needs unfettered filesystem access for per-account encrypted SQLite DBs
  in `userData`, auto-backup, and drag-drop attachment writes. These entitlements
  are for the Hardened Runtime (applied at sign time) so the app runs under
  `--options runtime` without breaking, and so macOS grants a settable
  "Local Network" privacy toggle in System Settings → Privacy & Security.

  Why each key:
    cs.allow-jit / cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        V8's JIT needs these under the Hardened Runtime, or the renderer crashes
        on startup. Electron's own frameworks are normally signed with these by
        electron-builder; our ad-hoc `afterSign` pass applies them to the whole
        bundle (ad-hoc = same entitlements everywhere is the documented
        community pattern and runs fine).
    cs.disable-library-validation
        We ship unpacked native modules (`better-sqlite3-multiple-ciphers.node`,
        the M4 FTS5 `.dylib` tokenizers) that are NOT signed by us or Apple.
        Hardened Runtime blocks unsigned libraries by default; this lets them
        load.
    network.client / network.server
        Outgoing sync/auth/SSE connections (client) + the local dev-tools /
        deep-link handler loopback (server). Also what the Local Network
        privacy prompt keys off for LAN access (self-hosted servers, sync
        between devices on the same network).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>